<graph edgedefault="directed"><node id="MEVALONATE DIPHOSPHATE DECARBOXYLASE (MDD)">
  <data key="d0">MEVALONATE DIPHOSPHATE DECARBOXYLASE (MDD)</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Enzyme that catalyzes the final ATP- and divalent-cation-dependent step of the mevalonate pathway, converting mevalonate 5-diphosphate into isopentenyl diphosphate with formation of ADP, CO2, and inorganic phosphate. | A related mevalonate decarboxylase enzyme context specifically cited in connection with substrate specificity and enzyme engineering.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.98</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257,701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
  <data key="d6">0.98</data>
  <data key="d7">MEVALONATE 5-PHOSPHATE DECARBOXYLASE</data>
</node>
<node id="MEVALONATE PATHWAY IN GRAM-POSITIVE BACTERIA">
  <data key="d0">MEVALONATE PATHWAY IN GRAM-POSITIVE BACTERIA</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Biosynthetic pathway used by pathogenic Gram-positive organisms such as Staphylococcus, Streptococcus, and Enterococcus species to generate the essential isoprenoid precursor IPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="GHMP KINASE SUPERFAMILY MEMBERSHIP">
  <data key="d0">GHMP KINASE SUPERFAMILY MEMBERSHIP</data>
  <data key="d1">DOMAIN</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural and functional superfamily context for MDD; GHMP kinases share nucleotide-dependent phosphoryl-transfer chemistry and conserved substrate/nucleotide-binding architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="SUBSTRATE AND NUCLEOTIDE BINDING SITES">
  <data key="d0">SUBSTRATE AND NUCLEOTIDE BINDING SITES</data>
  <data key="d1">POCKET_GEOMETRY</data>
  <data key="d2">Catalytic pockets that bind mevalonate 5-diphosphate and ATP/Mg2+; these sites are highlighted as targets for broad-spectrum antimicrobial inhibitor development.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MG2+-ATP-BOUND TERNARY COMPLEX STATE">
  <data key="d0">MG2+-ATP-BOUND TERNARY COMPLEX STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Catalytically relevant liganded state of MDD containing substrate and nucleotide components, captured in wild-type and mutant crystal structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="WILD-TYPE AND S192A/D283A TERNARY CRYSTAL STRUCTURES">
  <data key="d0">WILD-TYPE AND S192A/D283A TERNARY CRYSTAL STRUCTURES</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Reported structural evidence comprising wild-type MDD and mutant ternary complexes, used to infer nucleotide binding and catalytic organization.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ASP283">
  <data key="d0">ASP283</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Catalytic aspartate residue identified as the general base that deprotonates the C3 hydroxyl of mevalonate 5-diphosphate during the MDD reaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_3,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_4,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_7,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_8,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_9,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d6">1.0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="SER192">
  <data key="d0">SER192</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Residue examined through the S192A mutant ternary complex; included as a structurally probed position in the catalytic complex.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.62</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_3,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_5,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_7,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_9,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d6">0.93</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAPP C3-HYDROXYL DEPROTONATION">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP C3-HYDROXYL DEPROTONATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Initial catalytic step in which the C3 hydroxyl group of mevalonate 5-diphosphate is deprotonated, enabling subsequent phosphoryl transfer. | Catalytic-base step in which Asp283 assists removal of the C3-hydroxyl proton from MVAPP, preparing the acceptor for phosphoryl transfer.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_8,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d6">0.95</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
  <data key="d7">MVAPP C3-HYDROXYL PROTON ABSTRACTION</data>
</node>
<node id="3-PHOSPHO-MVAPP ADDUCT FORMATION">
  <data key="d0">3-PHOSPHO-MVAPP ADDUCT FORMATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorylation step that produces a putative metastable 3-phospho-mevalonate diphosphate intermediate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="C3 CARBOCATION INTERMEDIATE FORMATION">
  <data key="d0">C3 CARBOCATION INTERMEDIATE FORMATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanistic step in which departure of the transferred phosphate from the 3-phospho-MVAPP adduct generates a carbocation intermediate at C3.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="IRREVERSIBLE MVAPP DECARBOXYLATION TO IPP">
  <data key="d0">IRREVERSIBLE MVAPP DECARBOXYLATION TO IPP</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Overall MDD-catalyzed reaction converting mevalonate 5-diphosphate into isopentenyl diphosphate, CO2, ADP, and inorganic phosphate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.96</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP HYDROLYSIS COUPLED TO DECARBOXYLATION">
  <data key="d0">ATP HYDROLYSIS COUPLED TO DECARBOXYLATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent nucleotide reaction in which ATP is converted to ADP and inorganic phosphate during MDD-catalyzed MVAPP decarboxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.87</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ANTIMICROBIAL TARGETING OF MDD ACTIVE-SITE POCKETS">
  <data key="d0">ANTIMICROBIAL TARGETING OF MDD ACTIVE-SITE POCKETS</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Therapeutic rationale that MDD substrate and nucleotide binding sites are attractive antimicrobial targets because IPP synthesis via the mevalonate pathway is essential in many Gram-positive bacteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.81</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_0</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MDD">
  <data key="d0">MDD</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Mevalonate diphosphate decarboxylase enzyme discussed in the excerpt. It catalyzes MVAPP conversion to IPP and is analyzed by mutagenesis and crystallography. | Mevalonate-pathway enzyme abbreviated as MDD; also referenced in a cited kinetic study as mevalonate 5-diphosphate decarboxylase. | The enzyme system repeatedly referenced in structural, biochemical, mutational, inhibition, and QM/MM studies. The text includes mevalonate diphosphate or pyrophosphate decarboxylases from organisms such as Trypanosoma brucei, Staphylococcus aureus, and mouse. | The enzyme context for the cited structural and simulation studies. Because the valid entity-type list does not include a protein or enzyme type, the enzyme is represented as a mechanistic context for nucleotide binding and catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257,701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe,8b12a598-340d-5340-aebf-b31ea016d294</data>
  <data key="d6">0.96</data>
  <data key="d7">MEVALONATE DIPHOSPHATE DECARBOXYLASE</data>
</node>
<node id="GHMP KINASE FAMILY">
  <data key="d0">GHMP KINASE FAMILY</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Protein-family context for MDD; the family includes galactokinase, homoserine kinase, mevalonate kinase, and phosphomevalonate kinase and is characterized by a cone-shaped fold with a deep active-site cleft.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ARG144">
  <data key="d0">ARG144</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Catalytic arginine residue in MDD that interacts with the C1-carboxylate of MVAPP and promotes decarboxylation after formation of the C3 carbocation intermediate. Mutagenesis supports its catalytic importance.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.97</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_6,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_7,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="D283A">
  <data key="d0">D283A</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Asp283-to-alanine substitution in MDD. This mutation removes the catalytic Asp side chain and is represented in crystallographic datasets, including MVAPP- and DPGP-bound structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="S192A">
  <data key="d0">S192A</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Ser192-to-alanine substitution in MDD reported in several crystallographic datasets with DPGP or FMVAPP/ATPγS ligand conditions. No direct mechanistic role is specified in the excerpt.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.55</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1,a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_3</data>
  <data key="d6">0.87</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ARG144 MUTAGENESIS">
  <data key="d0">ARG144 MUTAGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Mutation-based experimental evidence probing the catalytic role of the Arg144 side chain. The excerpt reports that mutagenesis of the catalytic side chains caused an upward of 10-fold decrease in catalytic activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ASP283 MUTAGENESIS">
  <data key="d0">ASP283 MUTAGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Mutation-based experimental evidence probing the catalytic role of the Asp283 side chain. The D283A substitution is the explicit crystallographic mutant listed in the excerpt.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAPP">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Substrate/ligand in the described MDD catalytic pathway. Its C3-hydroxyl is deprotonated, its C1-carboxylate interacts with Arg144, and it is converted through phosphorylated and carbocationic states to IPP. | Substrate-like ligand bound in the MDDEF-MVAPP and closed MDDEF-MVAPP-ADPBeF3-Mg2+ structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257,701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
  <data key="d6">0.86</data>
  <data key="d7">MVAPP</data>
</node>
<node id="PHOSPHORYLATION">
  <data key="d0">PHOSPHORYLATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Catalytic step following C3-hydroxyl deprotonation, in which phosphoryl transfer produces the putative metastable 3-phospho-MVAPP adduct.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.89</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="3-PHOSPHO-MVAPP ADDUCT">
  <data key="d0">3-PHOSPHO-MVAPP ADDUCT</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Putative metastable phosphorylated intermediate formed after MVAPP C3-hydroxyl deprotonation and phosphorylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="DEPARTURE OF NEWLY TRANSFERRED PHOSPHORYL GROUP">
  <data key="d0">DEPARTURE OF NEWLY TRANSFERRED PHOSPHORYL GROUP</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanistic event in which the phosphoryl group newly transferred to MVAPP departs, leading to formation of a C3 carbocation intermediate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.83</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="C3 CARBOCATION INTERMEDIATE">
  <data key="d0">C3 CARBOCATION INTERMEDIATE</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Reactive carbocationic intermediate at the C3 position of MVAPP generated after departure of the newly transferred phosphoryl group. Its formation precedes decarboxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ARG144-C1-CARBOXYLATE INTERACTION">
  <data key="d0">ARG144-C1-CARBOXYLATE INTERACTION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Catalytically important interaction between Arg144 and the C1-carboxylate of MVAPP that promotes the decarboxylation step.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="DECARBOXYLATION">
  <data key="d0">DECARBOXYLATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Reaction step occurring after C3 carbocation formation and facilitated by interaction between Arg144 and the MVAPP C1-carboxylate; this step yields IPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP FORMATION">
  <data key="d0">IPP FORMATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Product-forming outcome of MVAPP decarboxylation, yielding IPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="CATALYTIC ACTIVITY">
  <data key="d0">CATALYTIC ACTIVITY</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Functional enzymatic readout used to evaluate the catalytic importance of Asp283 and Arg144 side chains.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="UPWARD OF 10-FOLD DECREASE IN CATALYTIC ACTIVITY">
  <data key="d0">UPWARD OF 10-FOLD DECREASE IN CATALYTIC ACTIVITY</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Reported reduction in MDD catalytic activity upon mutagenesis of the catalytic Asp and Arg side chains, supporting their functional roles in catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MUTAGENESIS STUDIES">
  <data key="d0">MUTAGENESIS STUDIES</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental evidence used to test the catalytic roles of Asp283 and Arg144 side chains. The studies showed an upward of 10-fold decrease in catalytic activity upon mutation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="DIFFRACTION DATA COLLECTION AND STRUCTURE REFINEMENT STATISTICS">
  <data key="d0">DIFFRACTION DATA COLLECTION AND STRUCTURE REFINEMENT STATISTICS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystallographic evidence table reporting data collection and refinement statistics for MDD, S192A MDD, and D283A MDD structures under different ligand conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
