</node>
<node id="PYMOL CΑ DISTANCE MEASUREMENTS">
  <data key="d0">PYMOL CΑ DISTANCE MEASUREMENTS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Distances measured from equivalent amino acid Cα atoms of each MDD active site using PyMol to quantify mutant-associated conformational changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="SITE-DIRECTED MUTANT FUNCTIONAL STUDIES">
  <data key="d0">SITE-DIRECTED MUTANT FUNCTIONAL STUDIES</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Functional studies of site-directed MDD mutants that are corroborated by the structural comparisons.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.74</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="GHMP KINASE SUBSTRATE-BINDING ORDER DIVERSITY">
  <data key="d0">GHMP KINASE SUBSTRATE-BINDING ORDER DIVERSITY</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Across GHMP kinase family members, substrate binding order varies: some bind the acceptor first, some bind ATP first, and others allow random substrate incorporation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.65</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MDD SEQUENTIAL ACCEPTOR-FIRST BINDING STATE">
  <data key="d0">MDD SEQUENTIAL ACCEPTOR-FIRST BINDING STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">MDD follows a sequential substrate-binding order in which the acceptor substrate binds before the ATP nucleotide donor.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP-FIRST OCCLUDED ACCEPTOR-BINDING STATE">
  <data key="d0">ATP-FIRST OCCLUDED ACCEPTOR-BINDING STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">If ATP binds first in MDD, the MVAPP acceptor-binding site is predicted and structurally demonstrated to become occluded by the nucleotide donor.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.85</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MDD PHOSPHATE-BINDING P-LOOP">
  <data key="d0">MDD PHOSPHATE-BINDING P-LOOP</data>
  <data key="d1">MOTIF</data>
  <data key="d2">The conserved phosphate-binding P-loop contains amino acids that make extensive interactions with the ATP triphosphoryl group and contribute to catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="IN-LINE GAMMA-PHOSPHORYL TRANSFER">
  <data key="d0">IN-LINE GAMMA-PHOSPHORYL TRANSFER</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">After MVAPP C3-hydroxyl activation, the gamma-phosphoryl group from the ATP donor is transferred in-line to the acceptor substrate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="CATALYTICALLY COMPETENT AMP-PNP-MG MODEL">
  <data key="d0">CATALYTICALLY COMPETENT AMP-PNP-MG MODEL</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">A modeled catalytically competent complex was generated by superimposing AMP-PNP-Mg from S. pneumoniae phosphomevalonate kinase onto ATPγS in the WT MDD ternary complex, placing the gamma-phosphoryl group approximately 3.4 Å from the MVAPP C3-hydroxyl.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="KINKED ATPΓS TRIPHOSPHORYL CONFORMATION">
  <data key="d0">KINKED ATPΓS TRIPHOSPHORYL CONFORMATION</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">In the ternary MDD cocrystal structures, ATPγS adopts a kinked triphosphoryl conformation, likely because ordered Mg2+ is absent.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.62</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="WT MDD TERNARY COCRYSTAL STRUCTURES">
  <data key="d0">WT MDD TERNARY COCRYSTAL STRUCTURES</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">The ternary cocrystal structures of wild-type MDD with acceptor and nucleotide analogues demonstrate that ATP binding can occlude the acceptor-binding site and support an acceptor-first binding order. | Structural evidence from ternary complexes showing ATP analog, Mg2+, and MVAPP/FMVAPP interactions with active-site residues, including hydrogen-bonding and proximity relationships.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
  <data key="d6">0.94</data>
  <data key="d7">MDD TERNARY CRYSTAL STRUCTURE</data>
</node>
<node id="D283A MDD-MVAPP CRYSTAL STRUCTURE">
  <data key="d0">D283A MDD-MVAPP CRYSTAL STRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">The D283A mutant structure bound to MVAPP shows that replacement of Asp283 disrupts optimal alignment of the MVAPP C3-hydroxyl group.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MDD MUTAGENESIS STUDIES">
  <data key="d0">MDD MUTAGENESIS STUDIES</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Previous mutagenesis experiments implicate Asp283 as the catalytic base responsible for deprotonating the MVAPP C3-hydroxyl.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="AVIAN LIVER MDD KINETIC CHARACTERIZATION">
  <data key="d0">AVIAN LIVER MDD KINETIC CHARACTERIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Kinetic characterization by Jabalquinto and Cardemil suggested a sequential substrate-binding order for avian liver MDD.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.7</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="PHOSPHATE-BINDING P-LOOP">
  <data key="d0">PHOSPHATE-BINDING P-LOOP</data>
  <data key="d1">MOTIF</data>
  <data key="d2">A conserved phosphate-binding loop in MDD that positions multiple residues to interact with ATP phosphoryl groups. | Loop motif that bends over the active site upon ATP binding, recognizes ATP phosphates, and helps position substrates for catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257,701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
  <data key="d6">0.95</data>
  <data key="d7">PHOSPHATE-BINDING LOOP</data>
</node>
<node id="GHMP KINASE FAMILY CONSERVATION CONTEXT">
  <data key="d0">GHMP KINASE FAMILY CONSERVATION CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">The broader GHMP kinase family context in which the MDD ATP-binding and phosphoryl-positioning residues are conserved, suggesting shared mechanistic features with enzymes such as mevalonate kinase.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ALA105">
  <data key="d0">ALA105</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Conserved P-loop residue in MDD implicated in interactions with the triphosphoryl group of ATP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.63</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="SER106">
  <data key="d0">SER106</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Conserved P-loop residue that interacts with the α-phosphoryl group of ATPγS and with FMVAPP, contributing to alignment of nucleotide and substrate in the active site.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="SER107">
  <data key="d0">SER107</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Conserved P-loop residue positioned to hydrogen bond with the β- and γ-phosphoryl groups of ATPγS; mutagenesis causes a very large reduction in kcat with only modest changes in ATP-Mg Kd and MVAPP KM.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.97</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ALA108">
  <data key="d0">ALA108</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Conserved P-loop residue listed among residues involved in extensive ATP triphosphoryl interactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.6</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="SER141">
  <data key="d0">SER141</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">MDD active-site residue proposed to play a prominent role in stabilizing the ATP γ-phosphoryl group during catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.81</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ARG193">
  <data key="d0">ARG193</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Prokaryote-associated active-site arginine that interacts with the phosphoryl groups of MVAPP and locks the acceptor in position in the active site.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.89</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP TRIPHOSPHORYL ALIGNMENT">
  <data key="d0">ATP TRIPHOSPHORYL ALIGNMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Productive positioning of ATP α-, β-, and γ-phosphoryl groups by conserved active-site residues, especially the P-loop serines.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="SUBSTRATE-NUCLEOTIDE CO-ALIGNMENT">
  <data key="d0">SUBSTRATE-NUCLEOTIDE CO-ALIGNMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">The aligned binding state in which MVAPP/FMVAPP and ATP or ATP analogs are simultaneously oriented in the MDD active-site funnel for catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAPP C3-PHOSPHORYL TRANSFER GEOMETRY">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP C3-PHOSPHORYL TRANSFER GEOMETRY</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Reactive configuration in which the ATP γ-phosphoryl group is positioned near the MVAPP/FMVAPP C3-hydroxyl; the AMP-PNP γ-phosphoryl is reported within 3.4 Å of the FMVAPP C3-hydroxyl.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.89</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAPP C1-CARBOXYLATE POSITIONING">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP C1-CARBOXYLATE POSITIONING</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">The catalytically relevant positioning of the MVAPP C1-carboxylate group, influenced by Arg144, that facilitates subsequent decarboxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAPP ACCEPTOR LOCKED BINDING POSE">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP ACCEPTOR LOCKED BINDING POSE</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">A constrained acceptor-binding state in which Arg193 contacts MVAPP phosphoryl groups and holds the acceptor in place in the active site.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAPP DECARBOXYLATION AFTER CARBOCATION INTERMEDIATE">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP DECARBOXYLATION AFTER CARBOCATION INTERMEDIATE</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Decarboxylation phase of the MDD mechanism occurring after formation of a carbocation intermediate, assisted by Arg144 interaction with the MVAPP carboxylate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.92</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="SER107 MUTAGENESIS PHENOTYPE">
  <data key="d0">SER107 MUTAGENESIS PHENOTYPE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Previous mutagenesis of Ser107 showed only modest effects on ATP-Mg Kd and MVAPP KM but a greater than 42000-fold decrease in kcat, supporting a catalytic role in ATP phosphoryl positioning rather than simple ligand binding.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP-MG DISSOCIATION CONSTANT KD">
  <data key="d0">ATP-MG DISSOCIATION CONSTANT KD</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Binding parameter for ATP-Mg; Ser107 mutation caused only modest changes in this value.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.66</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAPP MICHAELIS CONSTANT KM">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP MICHAELIS CONSTANT KM</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Michaelis constant for MVAPP; Ser107 mutation caused only modest changes in this value.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.65</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="AMP-PNP/FMVAPP 3.4 ANGSTROM PROXIMITY">
  <data key="d0">AMP-PNP/FMVAPP 3.4 ANGSTROM PROXIMITY</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural observation that the AMP-PNP γ-phosphoryl group lies within 3.4 Å of the C3-hydroxyl of FMVAPP, consistent with a productive phosphoryl-transfer geometry.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.87</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="D283A TERNARY COMPLEX">
  <data key="d0">D283A TERNARY COMPLEX</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Mutant ternary complex in which the Arg144 interaction with the MVAPP C1-carboxylate was disrupted, indicating coupling between Asp283-dependent active-site organization and Arg144-mediated carboxylate positioning.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.79</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="PROKARYOTE-SPECIFIC ARG193 OCCURRENCE">
  <data key="d0">PROKARYOTE-SPECIFIC ARG193 OCCURRENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Selectivity profile noting that Arg193 is found only in prokaryotic MDD enzymes, making it a potential contributor to organism-specific active-site architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
<node id="CRYSTALLOGRAPHIC DATA">
  <data key="d0">CRYSTALLOGRAPHIC DATA</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural evidence referenced as enabling structure-guided design of compounds against mevalonate-pathway enzymes.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">a9c7009e-9e90-565c-abe8-c021976be257</data>
</node>
