<node id="MVAPP-MEDIATED ATP-BINDING STEP">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP-MEDIATED ATP-BINDING STEP</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Substrate-binding step in which MVAPP binding promotes or enables ATP binding to MDDEF.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="ACTIVE-SITE LOOP-CLOSURE STEP">
  <data key="d0">ACTIVE-SITE LOOP-CLOSURE STEP</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Conformational step in which the β10-α4 loop and phosphate-binding loop close the active-site entrance before catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="DISSOCIATIVE PHOSPHORYL TRANSFER STEP">
  <data key="d0">DISSOCIATIVE PHOSPHORYL TRANSFER STEP</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Catalytic step in which phosphoryl transfer proceeds through dissociation to form a metaphosphate-like species before attachment to the MVAPP 3′ oxygen.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="METAPHOSPHATE INTERMEDIATE STATE">
  <data key="d0">METAPHOSPHATE INTERMEDIATE STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Transient phosphoryl-transfer state produced during dissociative phosphoryl transfer and subsequently attached to the 3′ oxygen of MVAPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="DEPHOSPHORYLATION AND DECARBOXYLATION STEP">
  <data key="d0">DEPHOSPHORYLATION AND DECARBOXYLATION STEP</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Late catalytic step following phosphoryl transfer that generates IPP, ADP, phosphate, and CO2. | Later MDD catalytic step converting the 3′-phosphate-MVAPP intermediate into the final product IPP; the conserved Asp is suggested to participate in this stage.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
  <data key="d6">0.95</data>
  <data key="d7">DEPHOSPHORYLATION AND DECARBOXYLATION TO IPP</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP, ADP, CO2, AND PHOSPHATE PRODUCTS">
  <data key="d0">IPP, ADP, CO2, AND PHOSPHATE PRODUCTS</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Final products released from MDDEF after dephosphorylation and decarboxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.8</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="LIGAND-BOUND MDDEF STRUCTURAL EVIDENCE">
  <data key="d0">LIGAND-BOUND MDDEF STRUCTURAL EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural observations showing defined β10-α4 and phosphate-binding loops in ligand-bound MDDEF states but not in apo-MDDEF.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="PUBLISHED APO-MDD STRUCTURE COMPARISON">
  <data key="d0">PUBLISHED APO-MDD STRUCTURE COMPARISON</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Comparison with published apo-MDD structures, including apo-MDDEF and apo-MDDSE, indicating variable ordering of β-α and phosphate-binding loops across the MDD family.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="MDD ACTIVE-SITE CATALYTIC DOMAIN">
  <data key="d0">MDD ACTIVE-SITE CATALYTIC DOMAIN</data>
  <data key="d1">DOMAIN</data>
  <data key="d2">Mevalonate diphosphate decarboxylase catalytic domain that binds MVAPP and ATP and catalyzes formation of IPP through phosphoryl-transfer and subsequent dephosphorylation/decarboxylation chemistry.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.96</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="CONSERVED CATALYTIC ASP RESIDUE IN MDDS">
  <data key="d0">CONSERVED CATALYTIC ASP RESIDUE IN MDDS</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Conserved active-site aspartate corresponding to D282 in MDD_EF, D281 in MDD_SS, and D283 in MDD_SE. The text argues that this residue has multiple roles beyond simple general-base deprotonation.</data>
  <data key="d3">1.0</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="MDD_EF MVAPP-ADP-BEF3-MG2+ STRUCTURAL COMPLEX">
  <data key="d0">MDD_EF MVAPP-ADP-BEF3-MG2+ STRUCTURAL COMPLEX</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Transition-state-analog-like structural observation containing MVAPP, ADP, BeF3-, and Mg2+ in MDD_EF, used to infer active-site geometry near D282 and the MVAPP 3′-oxygen.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="D282 CARBOXYLATE TO MVAPP 3′-OXYGEN PROXIMITY">
  <data key="d0">D282 CARBOXYLATE TO MVAPP 3′-OXYGEN PROXIMITY</data>
  <data key="d1">POCKET_GEOMETRY</data>
  <data key="d2">Active-site geometry in which the carboxyl group of D282 in MDD_EF lies approximately 3.3 Å from the 3′-oxygen of MVAPP, consistent with hydrogen-bonding control of substrate orientation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="PRODUCTIVE MVAPP 3′-OH ROTAMER">
  <data key="d0">PRODUCTIVE MVAPP 3′-OH ROTAMER</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed catalytically competent orientation of the MVAPP 3′-hydroxyl group in which its hydrogen and oxygen are positioned for effective phosphoryl transfer to or from ATP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP-DEPENDENT PHOSPHORYL TRANSFER TO MVAPP 3′-OH">
  <data key="d0">ATP-DEPENDENT PHOSPHORYL TRANSFER TO MVAPP 3′-OH</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Early MDD catalytic step in which the MVAPP 3′-OH attacks the γ-phosphate of ATP, producing a 3′-phosphate-MVAPP intermediate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="DISSOCIATIVE PHOSPHORYL-TRANSFER TRANSITION STATE">
  <data key="d0">DISSOCIATIVE PHOSPHORYL-TRANSFER TRANSITION STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed phosphoryl-transfer regime in which the substrate hydroxyl may remain protonated before and during the transition state, making precise hydrogen positioning important.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="MDD_SS D281T/D281V MUTANT PHENOTYPE">
  <data key="d0">MDD_SS D281T/D281V MUTANT PHENOTYPE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Mutants of MDD from Sulfolobus solfataricus produce the 3′-phosphate-MVAPP intermediate but not final IPP, indicating impairment of a later catalytic step.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.92</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="SLOWER INTERMEDIATE FORMATION IN MDD_SS D281T/D281V">
  <data key="d0">SLOWER INTERMEDIATE FORMATION IN MDD_SS D281T/D281V</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Qualitative kinetic observation that D281T or D281V mutants form 3′-phosphate-MVAPP much more slowly than wild-type MDD_SS forms IPP, suggesting the conserved Asp still contributes to phosphoryl transfer efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="MDD_SE D283A-MVAPP-ATPΓS STRUCTURE">
  <data key="d0">MDD_SE D283A-MVAPP-ATPΓS STRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Structure of enzymatically inactive MDD_SE D283A bound to MVAPP and ATPγS, reported as PDB 4DPW, showing an altered MVAPP binding pose.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.83</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="NONCATALYTIC MVAPP BINDING POSE">
  <data key="d0">NONCATALYTIC MVAPP BINDING POSE</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Altered substrate-binding state observed in the D283A mutant in which MVAPP is not positioned like in productive MDD-ligand complexes and its carboxyl group fails to interact with R144.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="R144 MVAPP-CARBOXYLATE BINDING RESIDUE">
  <data key="d0">R144 MVAPP-CARBOXYLATE BINDING RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Arginine residue that normally interacts with the carboxyl group of MVAPP, helping anchor substrate in the productive active-site pose.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="ACIDIC PH D282 PROTONATION CONTEXT">
  <data key="d0">ACIDIC PH D282 PROTONATION CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Under crystallization conditions near pH 4.6, D282 is estimated to remain about 80% unprotonated assuming a carboxyl pKa of 3.9; MDD enzymes also remain active above pH 3.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.79</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="TRANSFERRED PHOSPHATE AS POSSIBLE PROTON ACCEPTOR">
  <data key="d0">TRANSFERRED PHOSPHATE AS POSSIBLE PROTON ACCEPTOR</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypothesis that after formation of the 3′-phosphate-MVAPP intermediate, the transferred phosphate moiety may serve as the proton acceptor, although the exact acceptor remains uncertain.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.72</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="METAL-ION INFLUENCE ON TRANSITION STATE">
  <data key="d0">METAL-ION INFLUENCE ON TRANSITION STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Unresolved contextual factor: the role of metal ions in shaping or stabilizing the phosphoryl-transfer transition state remains unclear.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.62</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_13</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="K187A MUTANT">
  <data key="d0">K187A MUTANT</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">A lysine-to-alanine substitution mutant of MDDEF generated by modified site-directed mutagenesis for recombinant protein preparation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.55</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14,701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_19,701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_20,701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_21</data>
  <data key="d6">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="CLOSED MDDEF-METAL-MVAPP-ATP COMPLEX">
  <data key="d0">CLOSED MDDEF-METAL-MVAPP-ATP COMPLEX</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">A closed conformational state of MDDEF bound with metal ions, MVAPP, and ATP; this state organizes the active site for the proposed phosphate-transfer sequence.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.92</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="D282-CONFINED MVAPP 3′-OH GEOMETRY">
  <data key="d0">D282-CONFINED MVAPP 3′-OH GEOMETRY</data>
  <data key="d1">POCKET_GEOMETRY</data>
  <data key="d2">A local active-site arrangement in which D282 confines the 3′-OH group of MVAPP, creating a productive geometry for phosphate acceptance.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="TWO MAGNESIUM-ION BINDING SITES">
  <data key="d0">TWO MAGNESIUM-ION BINDING SITES</data>
  <data key="d1">POCKET_GEOMETRY</data>
  <data key="d2">Two metal-binding sites identified in MDDEF structures; the magnesium ions are implicated in catalysis, although their precise transition-state role remains unresolved.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="MOVEMENTS OF TWO UNRESTRICTED LOOPS AND THREE HELICES">
  <data key="d0">MOVEMENTS OF TWO UNRESTRICTED LOOPS AND THREE HELICES</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Coordinated conformational movements that sequentially build the MDDEF active site and help define a possible checkpoint for enzyme activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="POTENTIAL ENZYME-ACTIVITY CHECKPOINT">
  <data key="d0">POTENTIAL ENZYME-ACTIVITY CHECKPOINT</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">A proposed conformational checkpoint for MDDEF activity defined by loop and helix movements during active-site assembly.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="PHOSPHATE ACCEPTOR PREPARATION">
  <data key="d0">PHOSPHATE ACCEPTOR PREPARATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">The catalytic step in which the MVAPP 3′-OH is positioned by D282 in the closed complex to receive phosphate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="TRANSIENT METAPHOSPHATE STATE">
  <data key="d0">TRANSIENT METAPHOSPHATE STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">A proposed transition-state event in which metaphosphate transiently exists during phosphate transfer from ATP to MVAPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="3′-PHOSPHATE-MVAPP INTERMEDIATE FORMATION">
  <data key="d0">3′-PHOSPHATE-MVAPP INTERMEDIATE FORMATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">The catalytic step in which MVAPP receives metaphosphate to form a proposed 3′-phosphate-MVAPP intermediate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.87</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="PRODUCT FORMATION">
  <data key="d0">PRODUCT FORMATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">The final outcome of MDDEF catalysis, producing IPP, CO2, phosphate, and ADP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="CLOSED COMPLEX STRUCTURE">
  <data key="d0">CLOSED COMPLEX STRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural evidence from MDDEF bound with metal ions, MVAPP, and ATP that supports the proposed closed-state active-site arrangement and D282-mediated positioning of the MVAPP 3′-OH.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="PROPOSED FIG. 7B CHEMICAL-STEP MODEL">
  <data key="d0">PROPOSED FIG. 7B CHEMICAL-STEP MODEL</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">The authors' proposed mechanistic model for MDDEF catalysis, depicting phosphate acceptor preparation, transient metaphosphate formation, 3′-phosphate-MVAPP intermediate formation, and final dephosphorylation/decarboxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="STRUCTURES IN DIFFERENT MDDEF FORMS">
  <data key="d0">STRUCTURES IN DIFFERENT MDDEF FORMS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural observations across multiple MDDEF forms that support interpretation of substrate binding, catalysis, active-site assembly, magnesium binding, and structure-based drug-development applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.8</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="UNCLEAR METAL-ION INFLUENCE IN THE TRANSITION STATE">
  <data key="d0">UNCLEAR METAL-ION INFLUENCE IN THE TRANSITION STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">The unresolved mechanistic context that metal ions are implicated in catalysis, but their detailed influence during the transition state remains unclear.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.68</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="MODIFIED SITE-DIRECTED MUTAGENESIS METHOD">
  <data key="d0">MODIFIED SITE-DIRECTED MUTAGENESIS METHOD</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental method used to generate the K187A mutant construct of MDDEF.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.52</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="STRUCTURE-BASED DRUG-DEVELOPMENT CONTEXT">
  <data key="d0">STRUCTURE-BASED DRUG-DEVELOPMENT CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Application context in which the different MDDEF structures may serve as platforms for drug development, including possible relevance to multidrug-resistant Staphylococci, Streptococci, and Enterococci.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.58</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_14</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="MDDEF PROTEIN CONSTRUCT">
  <data key="d0">MDDEF PROTEIN CONSTRUCT</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Enterococcus faecalis MDD protein construct used for purification, sequence/structure annotation, and apo or ligand-bound crystallization experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_15</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="K187A SUBSTITUTION IN MDDEF">
  <data key="d0">K187A SUBSTITUTION IN MDDEF</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Lysine 187 mutated to alanine in the MDDEF construct; the mutant protein was expressed, purified, tag-cleaved, concentrated, and stored for downstream experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_15</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="N-TERMINAL HIS-TAG ON K187A CONSTRUCT">
  <data key="d0">N-TERMINAL HIS-TAG ON K187A CONSTRUCT</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Affinity tag present at the N terminus of the K187A MDDEF construct, enabling nickel affinity purification before removal by TEV protease.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.72</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_15</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="TEV-MEDIATED HIS-TAG CLEAVAGE">
  <data key="d0">TEV-MEDIATED HIS-TAG CLEAVAGE</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Proteolytic removal of the N-terminal His-tag from K187A MDDEF using recombinant TEV protease in dialysis buffer containing DTT and EDTA.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.66</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_15</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="NI2+-NTA AFFINITY PURIFICATION">
  <data key="d0">NI2+-NTA AFFINITY PURIFICATION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Nickel affinity purification workflow in which His-tagged K187A was trapped on Ni2+-NTA resin and eluted with imidazole; subsequent nickel resin passage removed His-tagged TEV and residual His-tagged K187A.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.73</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_15</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="SDS-PAGE ASSESSMENT OF K187A FRACTIONS">
  <data key="d0">SDS-PAGE ASSESSMENT OF K187A FRACTIONS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Gel-based examination of eluted fractions to identify fractions containing K187A protein for collection and dialysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.55</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_15</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="PURIFIED UNTAGGED K187A PREPARATION">
  <data key="d0">PURIFIED UNTAGGED K187A PREPARATION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Final K187A MDDEF protein preparation after His-tag cleavage, nickel resin cleanup, concentration to 8–10 mg/ml, and storage at −20 °C or −80 °C.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.75</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_15</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="SITTING-DROP AMMONIUM SULFATE CRYSTALLIZATION CONDITION">
  <data key="d0">SITTING-DROP AMMONIUM SULFATE CRYSTALLIZATION CONDITION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystallization condition for MDDEF using the sitting-drop method with 1.6 M ammonium sulfate and 50 mM sodium acetate at pH 4.6.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.68</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_15</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
