  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_18</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="CONSERVED LYSINE">
  <data key="d0">CONSERVED LYSINE</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">A conserved lysine residue in the catalytic domain of protein kinases. The cited study states that this lysine is actively involved in the phosphotransfer reaction and is not required for anchoring ATP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_18</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="PHOSPHORYL GROUP TRANSFER">
  <data key="d0">PHOSPHORYL GROUP TRANSFER</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">The catalytic transfer of a phosphoryl group, also referred to in the text as the phosphotransfer reaction. It is a core step in kinase-like mechanisms and in phosphorylation chemistry relevant to mevalonate diphosphate decarboxylase.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_18</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP ANCHORING">
  <data key="d0">ATP ANCHORING</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">A nucleotide-positioning or binding substep involving ATP. The cited kinase study distinguishes this function from the conserved lysine's role in phosphotransfer.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.64</data>
  <data key="d4">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe_chunk_18</data>
  <data key="d5">701d8434-7f71-5da9-bb94-e869d94e65fe</data>
</node>
