  <data key="d0">ACCUMULATION OF TOXIC MVA-PATHWAY INTERMEDIATES</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Undesired buildup of intermediates including IPP, FPP, and HMG-CoA during high-flux isoprenoid biosynthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP ACCUMULATION">
  <data key="d0">IPP ACCUMULATION</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Transient or sustained buildup of isopentenyl diphosphate, described as growth-inhibitory and especially important for isopentenol production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.96</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1,dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_10</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP-ASSOCIATED GROWTH INHIBITION">
  <data key="d0">IPP-ASSOCIATED GROWTH INHIBITION</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Observed deleterious cellular state in which IPP accumulation inhibits E. coli growth and limits biomass formation for high product titers.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="STRESS RESPONSES FROM PRENYL DIPHOSPHATE ACCUMULATION">
  <data key="d0">STRESS RESPONSES FROM PRENYL DIPHOSPHATE ACCUMULATION</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Generalized and condition-specific stress responses potentially induced by accumulation of prenyl diphosphates such as IPP or FPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP-DEPENDENT DEFENSE MECHANISMS">
  <data key="d0">ATP-DEPENDENT DEFENSE MECHANISMS</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Cellular stress-response processes such as DNA repair, ATPases, and ABC transporters that consume ATP and may compete with the MVA pathway.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.74</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ENERGETIC BURDEN OF THE MVA PATHWAY">
  <data key="d0">ENERGETIC BURDEN OF THE MVA PATHWAY</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">ATP and energy cost associated with MVA-pathway operation, particularly ATP-consuming diphosphate formation before hydrolysis in IPP-dependent isopentenol production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="NATIVE IPP ISOMERASE DIVERSION">
  <data key="d0">NATIVE IPP ISOMERASE DIVERSION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Diversion of IPP by native E. coli IPP isomerase Idi into endogenous isoprenoid pathways producing C10 and C15 prenyl diphosphates.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.83</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="GPP AND FPP FORMATION">
  <data key="d0">GPP AND FPP FORMATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Native pathway production of geranyl diphosphate and farnesyl diphosphate from IPP-derived intermediates, reducing carbon efficiency for isopentenol production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="CARBON UTILIZATION EFFICIENCY OF ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION">
  <data key="d0">CARBON UTILIZATION EFFICIENCY OF ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Efficiency with which carbon substrate is directed to isopentenol rather than diverted into competing products such as GPP and FPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.81</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP-DEPENDENT DIPHOSPHATE FORMATION AND HYDROLYSIS">
  <data key="d0">IPP-DEPENDENT DIPHOSPHATE FORMATION AND HYDROLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Energetically inefficient sequence in which ATP-consuming diphosphate formation precedes enzymatic hydrolysis to produce isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP DEPENDENCY OF THE MVA PATHWAY">
  <data key="d0">IPP DEPENDENCY OF THE MVA PATHWAY</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Pathway design feature in which IPP is an essential precursor for isopentenol production, creating toxicity, diversion, and energy-efficiency limitations.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP-BYPASS PATHWAYS">
  <data key="d0">IPP-BYPASS PATHWAYS</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineered pathways that decouple isopentenol production from IPP by using decarboxylation of MVA or MVA phosphate rather than IPP hydrolysis. | Engineered isopentenol pathway designed to bypass IPP by converting MVAP to IP followed by IP hydrolysis to isopentenol; reduces the pathway to five enzymes and two ATP molecules per isopentenol. | Engineered mevalonate pathway variant that bypasses IPP-dependent steps by converting MVAP to isopentenyl phosphate and then to isopentenol; designed to reduce IPP toxicity and ATP consumption relative to the conventional MVA pathway.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.96</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a,8b12a598-340d-5340-aebf-b31ea016d294</data>
  <data key="d6">0.96</data>
  <data key="d7">IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY II,IPP-BYPASS MVA PATHWAY</data>
</node>
<node id="MVA OR MVA PHOSPHATE DECARBOXYLATION TO ISOPENTENOL">
  <data key="d0">MVA OR MVA PHOSPHATE DECARBOXYLATION TO ISOPENTENOL</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Alternative IPP-bypass reaction logic in which mevalonate or mevalonate monophosphate is decarboxylated to produce isopentenol without requiring IPP as an essential precursor.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IMPROVED ISOPENTENOL TITER AND YIELD">
  <data key="d0">IMPROVED ISOPENTENOL TITER AND YIELD</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Reported performance improvement of the optimized pathway, including production of 2.2 g/L isopentenol from 10 g/L glucose, nearly 70% of apparent theoretical yield.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.77</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PUBLISHED EXPERIMENTAL OBSERVATIONS">
  <data key="d0">PUBLISHED EXPERIMENTAL OBSERVATIONS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited studies supporting pathway construction, optimization, IPP toxicity, stress responses, and improved isopentenol production metrics.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.72</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_1</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ORIGINAL MVA-TO-ISOPENTENOL PATHWAY">
  <data key="d0">ORIGINAL MVA-TO-ISOPENTENOL PATHWAY</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Canonical mevalonate-derived route to isopentenol that proceeds through IPP formation, requires seven enzymes, and consumes three ATP molecules per isopentenol molecule.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="MVA-TO-MVAPP PHOSPHORYLATION SEQUENCE">
  <data key="d0">MVA-TO-MVAPP PHOSPHORYLATION SEQUENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Two ATP-consuming kinase reactions catalyzed by mevalonate kinase and phosphomevalonate kinase convert MVA through MVAP to diphosphomevalonate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAPP-TO-IPP DECARBOXYLATION">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP-TO-IPP DECARBOXYLATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Native phosphomevalonate decarboxylase reaction that converts diphosphomevalonate to IPP. | Decarboxylation of mevalonate phosphate to isopentenyl phosphate, the reaction expected for PMDhv and for IPP-bypass pathway II.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.96</data>
  <data key="d7">MVAP-TO-IP DECARBOXYLATION</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP-TO-ISOPENTENOL DIPHOSPHATE REMOVAL">
  <data key="d0">IPP-TO-ISOPENTENOL DIPHOSPHATE REMOVAL</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Conversion of IPP to isopentenol by removal or hydrolysis of the diphosphate group.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="MVA-TO-ISOPENTENOL DECARBOXYLATIVE ELIMINATION">
  <data key="d0">MVA-TO-ISOPENTENOL DECARBOXYLATIVE ELIMINATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed direct conversion of MVA to isopentenol in IPP-bypass pathway I through ATP-driven decarboxylative elimination. | Proposed decarboxylative elimination reaction converting mevalonate phosphate to isopentenyl phosphate in IPP-bypass pathway II.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.94</data>
  <data key="d7">MVAP-TO-IP DECARBOXYLATIVE ELIMINATION</data>
</node>
<node id="IP-TO-ISOPENTENOL HYDROLYSIS">
  <data key="d0">IP-TO-ISOPENTENOL HYDROLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrolysis of isopentenyl phosphate to form isopentenol as the terminal step of IPP-bypass pathway II.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.87</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4,dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d6">0.88</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMD PROMISCUOUS DECARBOXYLASE SELECTIVITY">
  <data key="d0">PMD PROMISCUOUS DECARBOXYLASE SELECTIVITY</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypothesized substrate promiscuity of phosphomevalonate decarboxylase, especially the Saccharomyces cerevisiae enzyme, toward MVA and MVAP in addition to its native substrate MVAPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.92</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP-BYPASS ENERGETIC AND TOXICITY RATIONALE">
  <data key="d0">IPP-BYPASS ENERGETIC AND TOXICITY RATIONALE</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Design rationale that bypassing IPP lowers ATP consumption, reduces pathway complexity, may relieve IPP-associated toxicity, and may prevent loss of IPP flux from native pathways such as ubiquinone biosynthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ENGINEERED E. COLI ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION CONTEXT">
  <data key="d0">ENGINEERED E. COLI ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental host context in which optimized original and IPP-bypass pathways were constructed and expressed for isopentenol production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.79</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="LC-MS METABOLITE QUANTIFICATION">
  <data key="d0">LC-MS METABOLITE QUANTIFICATION</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Analytical evidence obtained by LC-MS using an Agilent 1200 HPLC system coupled to an Agilent 6210 time-of-flight mass spectrometer on a ZIC-HILIC column to quantify metabolites including IPP and IP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP AND IP CALIBRATION RANGE">
  <data key="d0">IPP AND IP CALIBRATION RANGE</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Eight-point calibration curves for IPP and IP quantification spanning 781.25 nM to 200 µM.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.72</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ZIC-HILIC ISOCRATIC ELUTION CONDITION">
  <data key="d0">ZIC-HILIC ISOCRATIC ELUTION CONDITION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Chromatographic condition using 64% acetonitrile with 50 mM ammonium acetate at 0.15 mL/min for metabolite elution.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.66</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_4</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphomevalonate decarboxylase from Saccharomyces cerevisiae, selected as the target PMD enzyme for engineered E. coli IPP-bypass pathways because it is widely used in isoprenoid production and was hypothesized to have promiscuous decarboxylase activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.98</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5,dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_16,dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_17</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC NATIVE MVAPP DECARBOXYLATION">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC NATIVE MVAPP DECARBOXYLATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Native PMDsc-catalyzed decarboxylation of mevalonate diphosphate, MVAPP, the enzyme's physiological substrate. | The nonnative decarboxylation reaction catalyzed by Saccharomyces cerevisiae phosphomevalonate decarboxylase (PMDsc) using mevalonate-5-phosphate (MVAP), linked in the study to isopentenol production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a,8b12a598-340d-5340-aebf-b31ea016d294</data>
  <data key="d6">0.95</data>
  <data key="d7">PMDSC MVAP DECARBOXYLATION</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC PROMISCUOUS MVA DECARBOXYLATION">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC PROMISCUOUS MVA DECARBOXYLATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypothesized and experimentally supported non-native PMDsc-catalyzed decarboxylation of mevalonate, MVA, enabling low-level production of isopentenol through IPP-bypass pathway I. | Promiscuous PMDsc-catalyzed decarboxylation of mevalonate phosphate, MVAP, which shows substantially higher activity than decarboxylation of unphosphorylated MVA.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.97</data>
  <data key="d7">PMDSC PROMISCUOUS MVAP DECARBOXYLATION</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC 3-HMB DECARBOXYLATION PRECEDENT">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC 3-HMB DECARBOXYLATION PRECEDENT</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Prior report that PMDsc converts 3-hydroxy-3-methylbutyrate to isobutene, supporting the broader hypothesis that PMDsc has promiscuous decarboxylase activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSE DIPHOSPHATE-BINDING FEATURE">
  <data key="d0">PMDSE DIPHOSPHATE-BINDING FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d1">POCKET_GEOMETRY</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural feature inferred from homologous PMD from Staphylococcus epidermis indicating that the substrate diphosphate group is important for substrate binding, even though it is not directly involved in catalytic decarboxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PHOSPHATE-ENHANCED PMDSC SUBSTRATE RECOGNITION">
  <data key="d0">PHOSPHATE-ENHANCED PMDSC SUBSTRATE RECOGNITION</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Selectivity pattern in which phosphorylated MVAP is better accepted by PMDsc than non-phosphorylated MVA, consistent with phosphate-mediated enhancement of substrate binding and catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAP KINETIC PARAMETERS FOR PMDSC">
  <data key="d0">MVAP KINETIC PARAMETERS FOR PMDSC</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Measured kinetic parameters for PMDsc toward MVAP: kcat = 0.14 s^-1 and Km = 0.99 mM. | Reported kinetic parameters for PMDsc toward its native substrate MVAPP: kcat = 4.9 s^-1 and Km = 123 µM; compared with MVAP, kcat is about 35-fold higher and Km about 8-fold lower.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.94</data>
  <data key="d7">MVAPP KINETIC PARAMETERS FOR PMDSC</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY I IN E. COLI">
  <data key="d0">IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY I IN E. COLI</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineered E. coli pathway expressing AtoB, HMGS, HMGR, and PMDsc to produce MVA and test whether PMDsc can directly decarboxylate MVA to support isopentenol production. | Engineered E. coli pathway expressing AtoB, HMGS, HMGR, MK, and PMDsc to produce MVAP and exploit PMDsc-catalyzed MVAP decarboxylation for isopentenol production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.89</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.96</data>
  <data key="d7">IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY II IN E. COLI</data>
</node>
<node id="ARK3A PATHWAY I ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION">
  <data key="d0">ARK3A PATHWAY I ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">In vivo evidence from strain ARK3a, expressing AtoB, HMGS, HMGR, and PMDsc, which produced 0.85 ± 0.18 mg/L isopentenol. | In vivo evidence from strain ARK2a, implementing pathway II, which produced 474.7 mg/L isopentenol, a 558-fold improvement over pathway I strain ARK3a and about 62.4% of the original pathway O titer.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.95</data>
  <data key="d7">ARK2A PATHWAY II ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION</data>
</node>
<node id="ARK3B NO-PMDSC CONTROL">
  <data key="d0">ARK3B NO-PMDSC CONTROL</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Control strain ARK3b expressed AtoB, HMGS, and HMGR without PMDsc and showed no detectable isopentenol, indicating PMDsc is required for pathway I isopentenol formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.87</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ISOPENTENOL PRODUCT IDENTITY EVIDENCE">
  <data key="d0">ISOPENTENOL PRODUCT IDENTITY EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Fragmentation pattern and retention time of the detected isopentenol peak in ARK3a matched a 3-methyl-3-buten-1-ol standard.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ENDOGENOUS E. COLI IP PHOSPHATASE ACTIVITY">
  <data key="d0">ENDOGENOUS E. COLI IP PHOSPHATASE ACTIVITY</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Inferred endogenous E. coli phosphatase-mediated hydrolysis of the phosphate group in isopentenyl phosphate, IP, allowing isopentenol production from pathway II without overexpressing an additional phosphatase.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.83</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_5</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY II ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION CONTEXT">
  <data key="d0">IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY II ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">The pathway context in which isopentenol was produced from IP without overexpressing an additional phosphatase, implying that endogenous E. coli activities can hydrolyze IP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ENDOGENOUS E. COLI IP-HYDROLYZING PHOSPHATASE ACTIVITY">
  <data key="d0">ENDOGENOUS E. COLI IP-HYDROLYZING PHOSPHATASE ACTIVITY</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Native E. coli phosphatase activity inferred to catalyze hydrolysis of isopentenyl phosphate to isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="NUDIX HYDROLASE HYPOTHESIS FOR IP HYDROLYSIS">
  <data key="d0">NUDIX HYDROLASE HYPOTHESIS FOR IP HYDROLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Initial hypothesis that promiscuous Nudix hydrolases such as E. coli NudB or B. subtilis NudF might hydrolyze IP to isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="NUDB-CATALYZED IPP TO IP HYDROLYSIS">
  <data key="d0">NUDB-CATALYZED IPP TO IP HYDROLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Purified E. coli NudB hydrolyzes IPP to the monophosphate intermediate IP in vitro. | B. subtilis NudF, previously identified as an IPP hydrolase, hydrolyzes IPP to IP but not to isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.91</data>
  <data key="d7">NUDF-CATALYZED IPP TO IP HYDROLYSIS</data>
</node>
<node id="NUDB LACK OF IP TO ISOPENTENOL HYDROLYSIS">
  <data key="d0">NUDB LACK OF IP TO ISOPENTENOL HYDROLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Purified NudB produced IP from IPP but did not further hydrolyze IP to isopentenol, even after 16 hours of incubation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="CRUDE E. COLI LYSATE IP TO ISOPENTENOL HYDROLYSIS">
  <data key="d0">CRUDE E. COLI LYSATE IP TO ISOPENTENOL HYDROLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Crude E. coli cell lysates hydrolyze IP to isopentenol in vitro, confirming that endogenous E. coli factors can perform this reaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.96</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PURIFIED NUDB IPP ASSAY EVIDENCE">
  <data key="d0">PURIFIED NUDB IPP ASSAY EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">In vitro assay with purified NudB and IPP detected IP but no isopentenol after extended 16-hour incubation. | In vitro assay showed B. subtilis NudF converts IPP to IP but not to isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.92</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.92</data>
  <data key="d7">PURIFIED NUDF IPP ASSAY EVIDENCE</data>
</node>
<node id="CRUDE LYSATE IP ASSAY EVIDENCE">
  <data key="d0">CRUDE LYSATE IP ASSAY EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">In vitro incubation of IP with crude E. coli lysates produced isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ORIGINAL IPP-DEPENDENT ISOPENTENOL PATHWAY INTERPRETATION">
  <data key="d0">ORIGINAL IPP-DEPENDENT ISOPENTENOL PATHWAY INTERPRETATION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">The prior pathway likely involves NudB-mediated conversion of IPP to IP, followed by endogenous E. coli phosphatase-mediated conversion of IP to isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PHOSPHATASE SINGLE-GENE KNOCKOUT SCREEN">
  <data key="d0">PHOSPHATASE SINGLE-GENE KNOCKOUT SCREEN</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">A screen of 36 monophosphatase single-gene knockout strains from the Keio collection tested lysate-dependent IP hydrolysis to identify endogenous IP-hydrolyzing enzymes.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="AGP KNOCKOUT REDUCED IP HYDROLYSIS">
  <data key="d0">AGP KNOCKOUT REDUCED IP HYDROLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell lysate from the agp single-gene knockout strain produced significantly less isopentenol from IP than the average level among tested strains. | Cell lysate from the yqaB single-gene knockout strain produced significantly less isopentenol from IP than the average level among tested strains.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.79</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.79</data>
  <data key="d7">YQAB KNOCKOUT REDUCED IP HYDROLYSIS</data>
</node>
<node id="REDUCED ISOPENTENOL LEVELS AFTER 26-HOUR KNOCKOUT INCUBATION">
  <data key="d0">REDUCED ISOPENTENOL LEVELS AFTER 26-HOUR KNOCKOUT INCUBATION</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">After 26 hours, agp, aphA, and yqaB knockout lysates produced only 62%, 64%, and 82% of the wild-type isopentenol level, respectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.83</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="RESIDUAL IP HYDROLYSIS ACROSS ALL KNOCKOUT LYSATES">
  <data key="d0">RESIDUAL IP HYDROLYSIS ACROSS ALL KNOCKOUT LYSATES</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">All 36 phosphatase knockout lysates retained some IP-hydrolyzing activity, indicating distributed or redundant endogenous phosphatase capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="APHA OVEREXPRESSION HIGH IP-HYDROLYSIS STATE">
  <data key="d0">APHA OVEREXPRESSION HIGH IP-HYDROLYSIS STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Overexpression of aphA in wild-type and aphA-knockout backgrounds caused rapid, essentially complete conversion of IP to isopentenol immediately after IP addition.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="AGP AND YQAB OVEREXPRESSION SLOWER IP HYDROLYSIS">
  <data key="d0">AGP AND YQAB OVEREXPRESSION SLOWER IP HYDROLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Overexpression of agp or yqaB increased IP hydrolysis more slowly than aphA overexpression, suggesting weaker contribution relative to aphA.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="APHA DOMINANT CONTRIBUTION TO IP HYDROLYSIS">
  <data key="d0">APHA DOMINANT CONTRIBUTION TO IP HYDROLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">The combined knockout and overexpression results suggest that aphA is a major endogenous contributor to IP hydrolysis in E. coli.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PREVIOUS MONOPHOSPHATE-BASED NUDB ASSAY LIMITATION">
  <data key="d0">PREVIOUS MONOPHOSPHATE-BASED NUDB ASSAY LIMITATION</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Earlier NudB assays detected monophosphate formation but did not identify the final product by LCMS or GCMS, leaving unresolved whether IPP hydrolysis stopped at IP or proceeded to isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.71</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_6</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="APHA-OVEREXPRESSION CONTEXT">
  <data key="d0">APHA-OVEREXPRESSION CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental context in which aphA was overexpressed in wild-type and aphA-knockout strains to assess conversion of isopentenyl phosphate (IP) to isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="APHA HIGH IP-HYDROLYSIS PROFILE">
  <data key="d0">APHA HIGH IP-HYDROLYSIS PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">AphA shows much higher IP-hydrolysis activity than Agp and YqaB, with complete conversion of IP to isopentenol immediately after IP addition.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="AGP AND YQAB SLOW IP-HYDROLYSIS PROFILE">
  <data key="d0">AGP AND YQAB SLOW IP-HYDROLYSIS PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Overexpression of agp or yqaB produces relatively slower IP hydrolysis rates compared with aphA.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.69</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="FIGURE 4C IP HYDROLYSIS EVIDENCE">
  <data key="d0">FIGURE 4C IP HYDROLYSIS EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 4C reports rapid complete IP conversion with aphA overexpression and slower IP hydrolysis with agp or yqaB overexpression.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PATHWAY II WITH APHA COEXPRESSION CONTEXT">
  <data key="d0">PATHWAY II WITH APHA COEXPRESSION CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain ARK2aa context containing pathway II enzymes AtoB, HMGS, HMGR, MK, PMDsc and coexpressed aphA.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ARK2AA ISOPENTENOL TITER EVIDENCE">
  <data key="d0">ARK2AA ISOPENTENOL TITER EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Coexpression of aphA with pathway II produced 705 mg/L isopentenol after 31 hours, about 20% higher than the corresponding strain without aphA overexpression.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.74</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC MVAP PROMISCUOUS ACTIVITY PROFILE">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC MVAP PROMISCUOUS ACTIVITY PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Promiscuous activity of Saccharomyces cerevisiae PMD toward the non-native substrate mevalonate monophosphate (MVAP), targeted for engineering to improve pathway flux. | Interpretation that PMDsc's promiscuous activity toward MVAP is the current bottleneck in IPP-bypass pathway II for isopentenol production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.95</data>
  <data key="d7">PMDSC MVAP PROMISCUOUS ACTIVITY BOTTLENECK</data>
</node>
<node id="PMD-MEDIATED MVAP REACTION">
  <data key="d0">PMD-MEDIATED MVAP REACTION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-native PMD-catalyzed reaction involving MVAP in the IPP-bypass pathway II.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.87</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
