<node id="PMDSC T209">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC T209</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Neutral threonine residue at position 209 in PMDsc, homologous to PMDse R193; proposed to contribute to broader promiscuity toward less-native substrates such as MVAP. | Threonine 209 in PMDsc; structurally parallel to Arg193 of PMDse but tolerated substitution by many amino acids, indicating weak selection pressure and limited effect on MVAP decarboxylation activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7,dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_19</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a,8b12a598-340d-5340-aebf-b31ea016d294</data>
  <data key="d6">0.93</data>
  <data key="d7">PMDSC THR209</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSE HIGH MVAPP SPECIFICITY PROFILE">
  <data key="d0">PMDSE HIGH MVAPP SPECIFICITY PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">PMDse exhibits higher specificity for the native substrate MVAPP, associated with R193-mediated stabilization of the phosphate moiety.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC LOWER MVAPP SPECIFICITY PROFILE">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC LOWER MVAPP SPECIFICITY PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">PMDsc has lower native-substrate specificity than PMDse and appears more permissive toward non-native MVAP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.81</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC KCAT/KM FOR MVAPP">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC KCAT/KM FOR MVAPP</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Reported catalytic efficiency for PMDsc toward MVAPP: 4.0 × 10^4 s^-1 M^-1. | Reported catalytic efficiency for PMDse toward MVAPP: 6.5 × 10^5 s^-1 M^-1, indicating higher native-substrate specificity than PMDsc.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.75</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.78</data>
  <data key="d7">PMDSE KCAT/KM FOR MVAPP</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSE VERSUS PMDSC IN VIVO MVAP ACTIVITY EVIDENCE">
  <data key="d0">PMDSE VERSUS PMDSC IN VIVO MVAP ACTIVITY EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">In vivo isopentenol production with IPP-bypass pathway II was much lower using PMDse than PMDsc, 11.3 mg/L versus 474.7 mg/L after 24 hours, suggesting lower PMDse activity toward MVAP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_7</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC KCAT/KM FOR MVAP">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC KCAT/KM FOR MVAP</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Reported catalytic efficiency for PMDsc toward MVAP, approximately 4.0 × 10^4 s^-1 M^-1.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDHV MVAP-SPECIFIC SELECTIVITY PROFILE">
  <data key="d0">PMDHV MVAP-SPECIFIC SELECTIVITY PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Haloferax volcanii PMD is described as an archaeal MVAP-specific decarboxylase with activity toward MVAP and no activity toward MVAPP in prior characterization.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDHV MVAP KINETIC PARAMETERS">
  <data key="d0">PMDHV MVAP KINETIC PARAMETERS</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Previously reported PMDhv kinetics for MVAP: Km = 0.159 mM and kcat = 3.5 s^-1. | The kinetic quantities referenced by Table 2 for PMD wild type, PMDsc mutants, PMDse, and PMDhv. The provided text gives the table heading but not the numerical parameter values.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.9</data>
  <data key="d7">PMD KINETIC PARAMETERS</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDHV LACK OF MVAPP ACTIVITY">
  <data key="d0">PMDHV LACK OF MVAPP ACTIVITY</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Prior work reported no detectable activity of PMDhv toward MVAPP, distinguishing it from conventional PMDs.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ARCHAEAL MVA PATHWAY VIA IP PHOSPHORYLATION">
  <data key="d0">ARCHAEAL MVA PATHWAY VIA IP PHOSPHORYLATION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Archaeal mevalonate pathway context in which IPP is produced by phosphorylation of IP after MVAP decarboxylation, rather than by direct MVAPP decarboxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY II WITH PMDHV IN E. COLI">
  <data key="d0">IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY II WITH PMDHV IN E. COLI</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineered in vivo context expressing AtoB, HMGS, HMGR, MK, and PMDhv in strain ARK5 to test whether PMDhv can support isopentenol production through MVAP decarboxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="NO ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION IN ARK5">
  <data key="d0">NO ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTION IN ARK5</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">In vivo observation that expressing PMDhv with the four upstream bypass pathway II genes did not yield detectable isopentenol production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP-NADH COUPLED MVAP ASSAY FOR PMDHV">
  <data key="d0">ATP-NADH COUPLED MVAP ASSAY FOR PMDHV</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">In vitro assay used to detect PMDhv activity toward MVAP; no ATP hydrolysis activity was observed under the tested conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.87</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="H. VOLCANII NATIVE HIGH-SALT 42C EXPRESSION CONTEXT">
  <data key="d0">H. VOLCANII NATIVE HIGH-SALT 42C EXPRESSION CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Native-like expression conditions used in prior PMDhv characterization: H. volcanii at 42 °C in salt-rich Hv-YPC medium containing high NaCl, MgSO4·7H2O, MgCl2·6H2O, and KCl.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.8</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDHV INACTIVE-IN-E. COLI STATE">
  <data key="d0">PMDHV INACTIVE-IN-E. COLI STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed state in which PMDhv may be expressed but catalytically inactive in E. coli because E. coli growth temperature and salt conditions differ substantially from those of H. volcanii.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.89</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC POSITIVELY CHARGED ARGININE NEAR MVAP SITE">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC POSITIVELY CHARGED ARGININE NEAR MVAP SITE</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">A positively charged arginine in PMDsc, noted as potentially allowing promiscuity toward the less negatively charged substrate MVAP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC PHOSPHATE-BINDING INSERTION RESIDUES ABSENT FROM PMDHV">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC PHOSPHATE-BINDING INSERTION RESIDUES ABSENT FROM PMDHV</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Four PMDsc residues implicated in binding the phosphates of MVAPP are missing in PMDhv between T186 and E187.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="SERINE/ARGININE-RICH PMD PHOSPHATE-BINDING SEGMENT">
  <data key="d0">SERINE/ARGININE-RICH PMD PHOSPHATE-BINDING SEGMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">MOTIF</data>
  <data key="d2">A serine- and arginine-rich segment found in homologous conventional MVA-pathway PMDs that facilitates interaction with phosphoryl moieties of MVAPP and ATP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAPP BETA-PHOSPHATE-PROXIMAL ELECTROSTATIC ENVIRONMENT">
  <data key="d0">MVAPP BETA-PHOSPHATE-PROXIMAL ELECTROSTATIC ENVIRONMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">POCKET_GEOMETRY</data>
  <data key="d2">Local active-site electrostatic environment around the beta-phosphate region of MVAPP, proposed as a design target for improving PMDsc activity toward MVAP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.95</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC K22 S155 S208 T209 PHOSPHATE-PROXIMAL SITE">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC K22 S155 S208 T209 PHOSPHATE-PROXIMAL SITE</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Four PMDsc residues adjacent to the phosphate region of MVAPP selected for engineering based on structural analysis of PMDsc, PMDse, and PMDhv.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.93</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="SUBSTRATE CHARGE GRADIENT MVAPP MVAP MVA">
  <data key="d0">SUBSTRATE CHARGE GRADIENT MVAPP MVAP MVA</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Charge-based substrate comparison: MVAPP has net charge -4, MVAP has net charge -2, and MVA has net charge 0, motivating redesign of electrostatic complementarity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC CHARGE-COMPENSATING MUTATIONS K22M S155E S208E T209D">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC CHARGE-COMPENSATING MUTATIONS K22M S155E S208E T209D</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Designed PMDsc mutations intended to compensate for reduced substrate negative charge: S155E and S208E introduce glutamate, K22M neutralizes lysine, and T209D introduces aspartate.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC R74H AND I145F MUTATIONS">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC R74H AND I145F MUTATIONS</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Additional PMDsc mutations constructed because prior work showed that R74H and I145F increased activity in a similar decarboxylation reaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.74</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="3-HMB-TO-ISOBUTENE DECARBOXYLATION">
  <data key="d0">3-HMB-TO-ISOBUTENE DECARBOXYLATION</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Similar decarboxylation reaction converting 3-hydroxy-3-methylbutyrate to isobutene, previously used to identify beneficial PMDsc mutations.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.66</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="CELL-LYSATE ASSAY OF S155E AND S208E">
  <data key="d0">CELL-LYSATE ASSAY OF S155E AND S208E</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">In vitro assay using cell lysates of PMDsc S155E and S208E mutants; these serine-to-glutamate mutations did not produce detectable product in the described test.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="STRUCTURAL COMPARISON OF PMDSC PMDSE AND PMDHV">
  <data key="d0">STRUCTURAL COMPARISON OF PMDSC PMDSE AND PMDHV</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Comparative structural analysis of three PMDs used to identify phosphate-proximal residues and guide redesign of PMDsc substrate specificity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_8</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC ACTIVE-SITE VICINITY">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC ACTIVE-SITE VICINITY</data>
  <data key="d1">POCKET_GEOMETRY</data>
  <data key="d2">Region near the PMDsc active site containing residues R74 and I145; these residues are positioned near the active site but relatively far from the phosphate group of MVAPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.86</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="R74 RESIDUE">
  <data key="d0">R74 RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Arginine at position 74 in PMDsc, located near the active site; its distance from the phosphate group of MVAPP is reported as approximately 12.5 Å.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.78</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="I145 RESIDUE">
  <data key="d0">I145 RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d1">RESIDUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Isoleucine at position 145 in PMDsc, located near the active site; its distance from the phosphate group of MVAPP is reported as approximately 15.0 Å.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="R74H PMDSC MUTATION">
  <data key="d0">R74H PMDSC MUTATION</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Mutation replacing arginine 74 with histidine in PMDsc; previously shown to improve activity in 3-HMB decarboxylation and observed here to improve MVAP catalytic specificity and in vivo isopentenol productivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.96</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="I145F PMDSC MUTATION">
  <data key="d0">I145F PMDSC MUTATION</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Mutation replacing isoleucine 145 with phenylalanine in PMDsc; improves in vitro catalytic specificity toward MVAP but reduces isopentenol titer and productivity in vivo. | Serine-to-glutamate substitution at position 155; in vitro cell lysate assays detected no product, indicating strongly reduced PMDsc activity toward MVAP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.92</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.92</data>
  <data key="d7">S155E PMDSC MUTATION</data>
</node>
<node id="R74H/I145F DOUBLE PMDSC MUTATION">
  <data key="d0">R74H/I145F DOUBLE PMDSC MUTATION</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Combined R74H and I145F PMDsc mutant used in strain ARK2aM3; restored titer and productivity to levels comparable to R74H, suggesting dominance of the R74H effect over I145F in vivo.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.83</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="S208E PMDSC MUTATION">
  <data key="d0">S208E PMDSC MUTATION</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Serine-to-glutamate substitution at position 208; in vitro cell lysate assays detected no product, indicating strongly reduced PMDsc activity toward MVAP. | Mutation replacing threonine 209 with aspartate; kinetic parameters were similar to those of wild-type PMDsc.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.72</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.72</data>
  <data key="d7">T209D PMDSC MUTATION</data>
</node>
<node id="K22M PMDSC MUTATION">
  <data key="d0">K22M PMDSC MUTATION</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Mutation replacing lysine 22 with methionine; increased Km and decreased kcat relative to wild-type PMDsc.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.68</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="MVAP DECARBOXYLATION TO IP">
  <data key="d0">MVAP DECARBOXYLATION TO IP</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">IPP-bypass pathway II reaction in which PMDsc promiscuously decarboxylates mevalonate phosphate, MVAP, to isopentenyl phosphate, IP. | Target PMD-catalyzed decarboxylation of MVAP, engineered to increase flux toward IP formation and downstream isopentenol production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.98</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_9</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a,8b12a598-340d-5340-aebf-b31ea016d294</data>
  <data key="d6">0.98</data>
  <data key="d7">MVAP DECARBOXYLATION STEP</data>
</node>
<node id="3-HMB DECARBOXYLATION TO ISOBUTENE">
  <data key="d0">3-HMB DECARBOXYLATION TO ISOBUTENE</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Related decarboxylation reaction in which PMDsc mutants R74H and I145F had previously been shown to increase activity toward 3-hydroxy-3-methylbutyrate, producing isobutene.</data>
