  <data key="d0">LIMITED AERATION CONDITION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Low-aeration fermentation condition produced by incubating induced cultures at 30 rpm shaking speed.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="HIGHER AERATION CONDITION">
  <data key="d0">HIGHER AERATION CONDITION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference high-aeration fermentation condition produced by incubating induced cultures at 200 rpm shaking speed.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP-LIMITED FERMENTATION STATE">
  <data key="d0">ATP-LIMITED FERMENTATION STATE</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Physiological state expected under poor aeration because oxidative phosphorylation is constrained by limited oxygen availability.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="OXIDATIVE PHOSPHORYLATION ATP SUPPLY">
  <data key="d0">OXIDATIVE PHOSPHORYLATION ATP SUPPLY</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">ATP-generating process that uses oxygen as the final electron acceptor and is most efficient under sufficient aeration.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="LIMITED-AERATION ISOPENTENOL TITER RETENTION">
  <data key="d0">LIMITED-AERATION ISOPENTENOL TITER RETENTION</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Relative production under 30 rpm compared with 200 rpm: ARK1a produced only 22% of its 200 rpm titer after 16 h, whereas ARK2a produced 40% at 16 h and up to 60% at 24 h.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="OD600 GROWTH UNDER POOR AERATION">
  <data key="d0">OD600 GROWTH UNDER POOR AERATION</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell growth measurement showing that ARK1a had higher OD600 than ARK2a under 30 rpm, despite producing substantially less isopentenol.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.77</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="REDUCED CARBON FLUX TO MVA PATHWAY">
  <data key="d0">REDUCED CARBON FLUX TO MVA PATHWAY</data>
  <data key="d1">DYNAMICAL_STATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed response of ARK1a under ATP limitation, in which flux to the ATP-consuming heterologous MVA pathway is reduced to conserve energy for growth-related cellular processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="FIGURE 8 MK-EXPRESSION EVIDENCE">
  <data key="d0">FIGURE 8 MK-EXPRESSION EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental evidence showing that upstream pathway flux balance affects growth and isopentenol production and that ARK1f is sensitive to MK expression level while pathway II strains are not.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.83</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="FIGURE 9 AERATION EVIDENCE">
  <data key="d0">FIGURE 9 AERATION EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental evidence comparing isopentenol production and growth at 30 rpm and 200 rpm for pathway O strain ARK1a and pathway II strain ARK2a.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.9</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_11</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP-SAVING IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY II">
  <data key="d0">ATP-SAVING IPP-BYPASS PATHWAY II</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineered isopentenol-production route that bypasses IPP formation and reduces ATP consumption relative to the original MVA pathway. It is described as robust under limited aeration and as a promising energetically favored platform for producing isopentenol, isoprene, and related C5 compounds.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.97</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ORIGINAL MVA PATHWAY">
  <data key="d0">ORIGINAL MVA PATHWAY</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference mevalonate-based production pathway for isopentenol. In this text it is contrasted with pathway II because it is linked to IPP accumulation and unnecessary ATP consumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP-CONSUMING HETEROLOGOUS PATHWAY">
  <data key="d0">ATP-CONSUMING HETEROLOGOUS PATHWAY</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Energy-demanding heterologous route whose ATP use can become burdensome when cellular ATP supply is restricted by poor aeration.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.74</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMDSC BYPASS ACTIVITY TOWARD MVAP">
  <data key="d0">PMDSC BYPASS ACTIVITY TOWARD MVAP</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Previously unidentified promiscuous PMDsc activity toward MVAP that contributes to IPP-bypass pathway operation. The text identifies PMD activity in the bypass pathways as rate-limiting and therefore a major target for engineering.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.94</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="APHA BYPASS ACTIVITY">
  <data key="d0">APHA BYPASS ACTIVITY</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Previously unidentified AphA activity implemented together with PMDsc activity to enable considerable isopentenol production through pathway II without producing IPP.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.84</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMD RATE-LIMITING STEP">
  <data key="d0">PMD RATE-LIMITING STEP</data>
  <data key="d1">REACTION_STEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Bottleneck step in the IPP-bypass pathways attributed to promiscuous PMD activity. Its identification provides a clear engineering opportunity for improving isopentenol yield and productivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.96</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMD PROMISCUITY">
  <data key="d0">PMD PROMISCUITY</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Functional breadth of PMD substrate activity, especially activity toward noncanonical MVAP in the bypass pathway. The text proposes engineering PMD promiscuity or identifying homologous enzymes as routes to improve production.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.88</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMD MUTANTS WITH IMPROVED ACTIVITY TOWARD MVAP">
  <data key="d0">PMD MUTANTS WITH IMPROVED ACTIVITY TOWARD MVAP</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineered PMD variants constructed with improved activity toward MVAP. Specific substitutions are not provided in the excerpt, but these variants represent sequence-level changes intended to improve the limiting PMD activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PMD HOMOLOGOUS ENZYMES">
  <data key="d0">PMD HOMOLOGOUS ENZYMES</data>
  <data key="d1">SEQUENCE_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Potential homologous enzymes suggested as future engineering targets for improving PMD-like bypass activity and increasing isopentenol production performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.67</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ISOPENTENOL TITERS">
  <data key="d0">ISOPENTENOL TITERS</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Production output metric for isopentenol. The text reports considerable titers through pathway II and similar or higher production under limited aeration compared with the relevant control pathway. | Production output measured after 48 h induction in mg/L; values ranged from 22 ± 8 mg/L for K22Y to about 1079-1080 mg/L for R74H-R147K-M212Q.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.92</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a,8b12a598-340d-5340-aebf-b31ea016d294</data>
  <data key="d6">0.96</data>
  <data key="d7">48 H ISOPENTENOL TITER</data>
</node>
<node id="ISOPENTENOL YIELD">
  <data key="d0">ISOPENTENOL YIELD</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Production efficiency metric expected to increase if the PMD rate-limiting bottleneck is relieved through PMD promiscuity engineering or homologous enzyme discovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTIVITY">
  <data key="d0">ISOPENTENOL PRODUCTIVITY</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Rate-oriented production metric expected to improve through engineering of the rate-limiting PMD activity in the bypass pathways.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.8</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP DEMAND">
  <data key="d0">ATP DEMAND</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Energetic requirement of the production pathway. Reduced ATP demand in pathway II is proposed to benefit the host under oxygen-limited or limited-aeration conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.89</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP SUPPLY">
  <data key="d0">ATP SUPPLY</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Available cellular ATP supply, which becomes limited under poor aeration and may restrict flux through ATP-consuming heterologous pathways.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.77</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="CARBON FLUX TO THE MVA PATHWAY">
  <data key="d0">CARBON FLUX TO THE MVA PATHWAY</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Metabolic flux directed into the MVA pathway. The text suggests that ARK1a may reduce this flux when ATP supply is limited to reduce energy usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.74</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="MVA LEVEL">
  <data key="d0">MVA LEVEL</data>
  <data key="d1">KINETIC_PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d2">Level of mevalonate-pathway intermediate used to evaluate pathway robustness. Pathway II is described as insensitive to MVA level.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.68</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="PATHWAY II MVA/MK INSENSITIVITY PROFILE">
  <data key="d0">PATHWAY II MVA/MK INSENSITIVITY PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d1">SELECTIVITY_PROFILE</data>
  <data key="d2">Robustness profile in which IPP-bypass pathway II is insensitive to both MVA level and MK expression level, reducing the engineering burden associated with upstream pathway balancing.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="IPP ACCUMULATION AND TOXICITY">
  <data key="d0">IPP ACCUMULATION AND TOXICITY</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Undesired intracellular condition associated with IPP production in the original pathway. Pathway II reduces this limitation by avoiding IPP formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.82</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="UPSTREAM MVA PATHWAY BALANCING BURDEN">
  <data key="d0">UPSTREAM MVA PATHWAY BALANCING BURDEN</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineering burden of tuning upstream MVA pathway activity and MK expression to avoid intermediate imbalance and IPP toxicity. The burden is reduced by pathway II.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.73</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="LIMITED AERATION CONDITIONS">
  <data key="d0">LIMITED AERATION CONDITIONS</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Fermentation condition with restricted aeration and oxygen transfer, causing limited ATP supply. Pathway II is reported to be more competitive under this condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.91</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="OXYGEN-LIMITED CONDITIONS">
  <data key="d0">OXYGEN-LIMITED CONDITIONS</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Low-oxygen cultivation context related to limited aeration. Reduced ATP demand in pathway II is proposed to be beneficial under this condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.8</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="STRAIN ARK1A">
  <data key="d0">STRAIN ARK1A</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Production strain discussed as potentially reducing carbon flux to the MVA pathway when ATP supply is limited under poor aeration.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.66</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="STRAIN ARK2A WITH PATHWAY II">
  <data key="d0">STRAIN ARK2A WITH PATHWAY II</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Production strain carrying pathway II. It produced similar or higher isopentenol levels under limited aeration after 16 or 24 hours of fermentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.79</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="16 HRS OF FERMENTATION">
  <data key="d0">16 HRS OF FERMENTATION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Fermentation time point at which strain ARK2a with pathway II produced a similar isopentenol level under limited aeration. | Fermentation time point at which strain ARK2a with pathway II produced an even higher isopentenol level under limited aeration.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.58</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
  <data key="d6">0.6</data>
  <data key="d7">24 HRS OF FERMENTATION</data>
</node>
<node id="AERATION COSTS FOR LARGE-SCALE FERMENTATION">
  <data key="d0">AERATION COSTS FOR LARGE-SCALE FERMENTATION</data>
  <data key="d1">CONTEXT</data>
  <data key="d2">Operational cost context for industrial fermentation. The text argues that pathway II could enable more economical isopentenol production by lowering aeration requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.72</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="FIG. 9A AND SUPPLEMENTARY FIG. S8">
  <data key="d0">FIG. 9A AND SUPPLEMENTARY FIG. S8</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure evidence cited for the observation that pathway II maintains similar or higher isopentenol production under limited aeration at the tested fermentation time points.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.77</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
<node id="FERMENTATION ASSAY UNDER LIMITED AERATION">
  <data key="d0">FERMENTATION ASSAY UNDER LIMITED AERATION</data>
  <data key="d1">EVIDENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental fermentation comparison under restricted aeration used to evaluate isopentenol production behavior and pathway robustness.</data>
  <data key="d3">0.76</data>
  <data key="d4">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a_chunk_12</data>
  <data key="d5">dd599823-57fe-59b5-bf71-6ef7c22b4a8a</data>
</node>
